WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.104 --> 00:00:02.627
It looked like a good plan at the time.

2
00:00:02.628 --> 00:00:08.556
We were down South, in Alabama—Bill Driscoll and me—when a kidnapping idea struck us.

3
00:00:08.557 --> 00:00:12.527
Afterward, Bill called the plan "temporary insanity."

4
00:00:12.528 --> 00:00:17.885
There was a town, as flat as a flapjack, and it was called Summit, of course.

5
00:00:17.886 --> 00:00:23.585
A bunch of country hicks lived there, unremarkable folks as harmless as they were full of themselves.

6
00:00:23.586 --> 00:00:30.070
Bill and I had about six hundred dollars, and we needed two thousand more to pull off a sweet get-rich-quick scheme.

7
00:00:30.071 --> 00:00:34.263
With that money, we were going to sell some land out in western Illinois.

8
00:00:34.264 --> 00:00:38.739
Of course, it wasn't our land to sell, but that was the beauty of it!

9
00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:44.868
"A kidnapping job could work perfectly in this town," said Bill as we talked on the steps of the Summit Hotel.

10
00:00:44.869 --> 00:00:50.271
"In a big city, the newspapers would get a hold of it and stir things up with nosy reporters.

11
00:00:50.272 --> 00:00:54.443
But out here, Sam, there's nothing but philoprogenitiveness for miles!"

12
00:00:54.444 --> 00:00:56.413
"Philo—what?" I asked.

13
00:00:56.414 --> 00:00:58.633
"Philoprogenitiveness," he said.

14
00:00:58.634 --> 00:01:00.824
I looked at him like he was crazy.

15
00:01:00.825 --> 00:01:03.861
"It means loving one's children," he said.

16
00:01:03.862 --> 00:01:06.512
"Parents here will do anything for their kids.

17
00:01:06.513 --> 00:01:12.997
And with Summit being such a small town, the law can't come after us with anything better than an old sheriff and his dog."

18
00:01:12.998 --> 00:01:15.232
"Sounds good," I said.

19
00:01:15.233 --> 00:01:20.794
We selected our victim, the only child of a well-known citizen named Ebenezer Dorset.

20
00:01:20.795 --> 00:01:25.263
The father was a banker, respectable but tight with his wallet.

21
00:01:25.264 --> 00:01:31.778
"He's a stern, decent type," said Bill. "The kind that goes to church every Sunday and passes the collection plate.

22
00:01:31.779 --> 00:01:33.825
Without adding to it, of course."

23
00:01:33.826 --> 00:01:37.228
I shrugged. "At least he doesn't subtract from it."

24
00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:42.124
"Right you are! So that means he has money!" laughed Bill.

25
00:01:42.125 --> 00:01:47.595
The banker's son was a boy of ten, with dark freckles and hair the color of red pepper.

26
00:01:47.596 --> 00:01:53.405
It wouldn't take much for rich old Ebenezer to melt down and pay a ransom of two thousand dollars.

27
00:01:53.406 --> 00:01:55.619
That's what we figured, anyway.

28
00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:01.911
Two miles from Summit, there was a little mountain covered with shrubby cedar, which had a cave on one side.

29
00:02:01.912 --> 00:02:06.555
We secretly stored food and other items we'd need for the kidnapping in there.

30
00:02:06.556 --> 00:02:13.582
As soon as we were ready, we drove in a rented buggy past old Dorset's house one evening just after sundown.

31
00:02:13.583 --> 00:02:17.962
The banker's kid was outside, throwing rocks at a neighbor's kitten.

32
00:02:17.963 --> 00:02:24.481
"Hey, little boy!" said Bill, smiling sweetly. "Want some candy and a nice buggy ride?"

33
00:02:24.482 --> 00:02:28.746
Suddenly, the boy caught Bill neatly in the eye with a piece of brick.

34
00:02:28.747 --> 00:02:36.122
"Ow! That'll cost the old man an extra five hundred dollars," muttered Bill, getting out of the buggy.

35
00:02:36.123 --> 00:02:42.551
The boy wrestled like an angry bear, so we held him down in the bottom of the buggy and drove away quickly.

36
00:02:42.552 --> 00:02:46.102
We got to the cave and I hitched the horse to a tree.

37
00:02:46.103 --> 00:02:52.055
After dark, I drove back to Summit, returned the rented buggy, and walked back to the mountain.

38
00:02:52.056 --> 00:02:57.348
When I arrived, Bill was holding a wet rag on his black eye from the piece of brick.

39
00:02:57.349 --> 00:03:01.015
A fire burned behind the big rock at the entrance of the cave.

40
00:03:01.016 --> 00:03:07.950
The boy was sitting in front of a pot of boiling coffee, and he had two wild-turkey feathers stuck in his red hair.

41
00:03:07.951 --> 00:03:16.892
He pointed a sharpened stick at me and said, "Ha! Cursed paleface, do you dare enter the camp of Red Chief, the terror of the plains?"

42
00:03:16.893 --> 00:03:21.089
Bill rolled up his trousers and showed me his various shin wounds.

43
00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:26.954
"That kid kicks hard. But he's calm now. We were playing cowboys and Indians.

44
00:03:26.955 --> 00:03:33.131
I'm Old Hank the horse thief, Red Chief's captive. I'm to be hanged on Saturday."

45
00:03:33.132 --> 00:03:40.942
The boy was having a great time. Camping in a cave was so much fun that he forgot he was the captive.

46
00:03:40.943 --> 00:03:45.356
"You're Snake-eye, the spy," the boy said, pointing at me.

47
00:03:45.357 --> 00:03:50.499
"Snake-eye, eh?" I said. "Nice name, don't you think, Bill?"

48
00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:53.696
Bill held the rag over his eye and didn't answer.

49
00:03:53.697 --> 00:03:56.184
The boy stabbed his stick in the dirt.

50
00:03:56.185 --> 00:04:04.567
"When my braves return from the warpath, you will be broiled at the stake," he said all matter-of-fact, like burning folks up happened every day.

51
00:04:04.568 --> 00:04:13.609
We ate supper. The kid filled his mouth with bacon, bread, and gravy, then began to talk. He didn't stop, even to breathe.

52
00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:20.926
"I like this cave fine. I hate school. Rats ate sixteen of Jimmy Talbot's hen's eggs.

53
00:04:20.927 --> 00:04:29.155
Are there any real Indians in these woods? I want more gravy. Do trees make the wind blow? We have five puppies.

54
00:04:29.156 --> 00:04:33.639
What makes your nose so red, Hank? My father has lots of money.

55
00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:40.596
Are stars hot? I whipped Ed Walker twice, Saturday. I don't like girls. Do cows talk?

56
00:04:40.597 --> 00:04:47.560
Why are oranges round? Are there beds to sleep on in this cave? Amos Murray has six toes.

57
00:04:47.561 --> 00:04:52.907
A parrot can talk, but a monkey or a fish can't. How many does it take to make twelve?"

58
00:04:52.908 --> 00:04:56.756
Every few minutes, the boy would remember who he was supposed to be.

59
00:04:56.757 --> 00:05:01.074
He'd grab his spear and tiptoe to the mouth of the cave to hunt for enemies.

60
00:05:01.075 --> 00:05:09.320
Then out of the blue, he'd let loose with a blood-chilling war whoop: "AAAHHH-OOOUUU!"

61
00:05:09.321 --> 00:05:15.835
Terrified, Bill dropped his dinner plate and started shivering so hard, the beans jumped off his lap.

62
00:05:15.836 --> 00:05:19.263
"Red Chief," I said, "do you want to go home?"

63
00:05:19.264 --> 00:05:29.171
"Aw, do I have to?" he said. "I can't have fun at home. I like camping out. You won't take me back, Snake-eye, will you?"

64
00:05:29.172 --> 00:05:33.157
"Not right away," I said. "We'll stay here for a while."

65
00:05:33.158 --> 00:05:37.226
"All right!" he said. "I've never had so much fun."

66
00:05:37.227 --> 00:05:43.062
We got to bed late. We spread some blankets and quilts and put Red Chief between us.

67
00:05:43.063 --> 00:05:51.255
But we weren't afraid he'd run away. Heck, he kept us awake for three hours, jumping up, grabbing his stick, and screeching right into our ears.

68
00:05:51.256 --> 00:05:58.656
Every time a leaf rustled, he'd run to the cave entrance and shout to an imaginary band of outlaws, "You can't sneak up on us!"

69
00:05:58.657 --> 00:06:01.745
At last I fell into a troubled sleep.

70
00:06:01.746 --> 00:06:09.117
I dreamed I'd been kidnapped and chained to a tree by a short, fast-talking pirate with bright red hair.

